<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowContentChanged|typeViewScrolled"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagReportViewIds"
    android:notificationTimeout="0"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true" />
    <!--
      NOTE: android:accessibilityFlags="flagReportViewIds" is required.

      NotificationExpandService.kt identifies rows/buttons by their
      resource-id (e.g. "com.android.systemui:id/expandableNotificationRow",
      "android:id/expand_button", "android:id/group_child_count_number").
      Without flagReportViewIds, AccessibilityNodeInfo.getViewIdResourceName()
      silently returns null for every node (privacy restriction), so every
      resource-id comparison fails and the service never finds anything to
      click — with no crash or error to point at it. If you add more
      resource-id-based checks later, this flag is why they'd otherwise do
      nothing.

      NOTE: android:packageNames was removed on purpose.

      Individual notification rows inside the shade report their OWN
      AccessibilityEvent.packageName as the app that posted them (e.g.
      "com.whatsapp"), not "com.android.systemui" — even though they live
      inside SystemUI's window. With packageNames locked to systemui, the
      OS silently drops every content-changed event coming from a
      notification's own view (which is most of them), so the service only
      ever sees the initial WINDOW_STATE_CHANGED and effectively stops
      reacting to newly expanded/collapsed groups.

      We now filter in code instead, based on the ACTIVE WINDOW's package
      (rootInActiveWindow.packageName), which is always "com.android.systemui"
      while the shade is open regardless of which app's notification
      changed. See NotificationExpandService.onAccessibilityEvent().
    -->